            0.874
          ],
          "content": "Curiosity | Textbook of Science | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.093,
            0.883,
            0.136
          ],
          "content": "than our Sun. During daytime, due to the extreme brightness of the Sun, it is not possible to view other stars from Earth."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.277,
            0.186,
            0.74,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "The star nearest to us after the Sun is Proxima Centauri which lies at a distance of about 269000 au. This means that its distance is about 269000 times our distance to the Sun."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.777,
            0.166,
            0.849,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.762,
            0.226,
            0.864,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "**More to know!**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.213,
            0.352,
            0.408,
            0.513
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.344,
            0.327,
            0.531,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": "Are stars the only objects in the sky? Or, are there more objects which we may not hav e noticed?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.506,
            0.306,
            0.886,
            0.492
          ],
          "content": "There are many more objects in the sky. Our Earth, alongwith some of these objects, and the Sun together form our Solar System (Fig. 12.7). Most of these objects move around the Sun. The movement of an object around the Sun is called **revolution**."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.258,
            0.535,
            0.353,
            0.557
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Planets"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.568,
            0.885,
            0.673
          ],
          "content": "A planet is a large, nearly spherical object that revolves around the Sun. Our Earth is a planet as it revolves around the Sun as shown in Fig. 12.7. The Earth takes nearly one year to complete one revolution. Like the Earth, there are other planets that revolve around the Sun."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.746,
            0.313,
            0.811
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.814,
            0.327,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": "**More to know!**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.291,
            0.721,
            0.831,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "While revolving around the Sun, the Earth is also rotating about its axis. For one full rotation, the Earth takes about 24 hours which is called a day. Like the Earth, other planets also rotate about their axes while revolving around the Sun. You will learn more about this in the next Grade."
        },
        {
